-- title:
--   Stalkwise divisibility by a non-zero-divisor gives divisibility of sections
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme, let $W$ be an affine open subscheme of $X$ (an element of `X.affineOpens`, i.e. an open subset of $X$ together with the property that it is affine), and let $a, b$ be sections of the structure sheaf over $W$, i.e. elements of $\Gamma(X, W)$. Assume first that $b$ is a non-zero-divisor locally on affine pieces: for every affine open $V$ of $X$ with $V \le W$, the restriction of $b$ along the inclusion $V \le W$ lies in the submonoid of non-zero-divisors of $\Gamma(X, V)$. Assume second that $a$ is stalkwise a multiple of $b$: for every point $x$ of $X$ lying in $W$ there exists an element $u$ of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ with $a_x = u \cdot b_x$, where $a_x, b_x$ denote the images of $a$ and $b$ under the germ map $\Gamma(X, W) \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ at $x$. The conclusion is an inclusion of ideals of the ring $\Gamma(X, W)$: the ideal generated by $\{a\}$ is contained in the ideal generated by $\{b\}$, that is, $a$ is a multiple of $b$ in $\Gamma(X, W)$.
--
--   This is the passage from stalkwise divisibility to global divisibility of sections over an affine open, the uniqueness of the local quotients being guaranteed by the non-zero-divisor hypothesis so that they glue by the sheaf axiom. Applied in both directions it shows that stalkwise associate sections generate the same ideal, which is how the compatibility condition of a locally principal (Cartier) datum is verified; here it is used in the construction of an invertible presentation on a model of a modular curve over a place.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_span_singleton_le_span_singleton_of_forall_germ_eq_mul.lean

